import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro: the vocabulary week — originality found, delivery dropped","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/vocabulary-growth-2026-10-25.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/vocabulary-growth-2026-10-25.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/vocabulary-growth-2026-10-25.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-the-vocabulary-week-—-originality-found-delivery-dropped" tabindex="-1">Retro: the vocabulary week — originality found, delivery dropped <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-the-vocabulary-week-—-originality-found-delivery-dropped" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro: the vocabulary week — originality found, delivery dropped”">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-10-25 Window: 2026-10-19 18:12 (pitch tag <code>v0.1.0</code>) → 2026-10-25 Context: six days after the audited positioning deck, the stack moved further than the deck can express. This retro is the delta in one place: what grew, what was <em>learned</em>, what measurably broke, and what that forces next. Every claim names its artifact; anything not named here is not done.</p><h2 id="_1-the-ledger" tabindex="-1">1. The ledger <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-ledger" aria-label="Permalink to “1. The ledger”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>what</th><th>at <code>v0.1.0</code></th><th>now</th><th>artifact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>machine-shipped ledger names</td><td>12</td><td><strong>127</strong></td><td><code>mlopt_core.laws.provenance</code> (<code>len(MACHINE_SHIPPED)</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>— promoted classic laws</td><td>12</td><td>13</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>— minted prim folds</td><td>0</td><td><strong>57</strong> = 21 <code>synop_*</code> + 2 <code>synop2_*</code> + 34 <code>synop3_*</code></td><td><code>docs/reports/results_overnight_prim_depth3.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>— their <code>.expand</code> premises</td><td>0</td><td>57</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>distinct folds minted across runs</td><td>—</td><td><strong>71</strong></td><td><code>docs/reports/primitives_finds.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>shipped bank</td><td>—</td><td><strong>131</strong> <code>DEFAULT</code>, 143 <code>ALL_RULES</code>, 16 stored-unshipped</td><td><code>mlopt_core.laws</code></td></tr><tr><td>catalogue the sieve must beat</td><td>242</td><td><strong>265</strong></td><td><code>docs/results_novelty_sieve.md</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The bar rose with the bank: every new fold had to be underivable from a catalogue that kept absorbing the previous ones.</p><h2 id="_2-the-finding-—-originality-is-a-function-of-the-vocabulary" tabindex="-1">2. The finding — originality is a function of the vocabulary <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-the-finding-—-originality-is-a-function-of-the-vocabulary" aria-label="Permalink to “2. The finding — originality is a function of the vocabulary”">​</a></h2><p><code>docs/reports/primitives_finds.md</code> (2026-10-23, <code>zoo-prim</code>, 68 modules):</p><ul><li><strong>21/21</strong> admitted prim folds classify <strong>machine-original</strong>, <em>because the op is new</em> — &quot;novel by construction, since no catalog contains laws about an op that did not exist&quot;.</li><li><code>--prim-deep</code>: <strong>48/48</strong> admitted, all machine-original, disjoint names. <code>--prim-bootstrap</code> seats the shipped folds in the corpus e-graph as <em>vocabulary</em> (23 fired, +475 enodes) and 2 depth-2 folds become reachable; the depth-3 tier then lands 34 more.</li><li>Content: DeltaNet, Gated Linear Attention, Mamba selective scan and Griffin&#39;s RG-LRU share one &quot;sum of gated products&quot; core; one primitive (<code>bb8c54c201</code>) binds 14 modules. Found by corpus antiunification, not by being told.</li></ul><p><strong>The control, measured:</strong> the codegen domain&#39;s 44-case deep run mints 117, admits 97, and classifies <strong>0 machine-original</strong> — composed rules whose parents are both in the bank are theorems of the bank. So:</p><blockquote><p>Originality appears exactly where the vocabulary may grow, and vanishes where composition inside a fixed bank is all the mint can express.</p></blockquote><p>That sentence is now the thesis, and it is two-sided. It also <strong>re-opens and re-answers Q1</strong>: the honest zero on <code>zoo-real</code> (2026-10-21, 53 modules, 87 mints, 0 originals) was not search starvation — it was a <em>vocabulary</em> wall. The lever was never &quot;guarded composite parents&quot;.</p><h2 id="_3-the-catch-—-the-pay-leg-was-priced-never-measured" tabindex="-1">3. The catch — the pay leg was priced, never measured <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-the-catch-—-the-pay-leg-was-priced-never-measured" aria-label="Permalink to “3. The catch — the pay leg was priced, never measured”">​</a></h2><p><code>docs/reports/prim_delivery.md</code> (2026-10-24). The <code>synop*</code> family ships in <code>DEFAULT</code>; <code>shipped_primitives.register()</code> seats a Python closure that walks the definitional premise per node.</p><ul><li>measured wall clock, fp64 eager, small and large: <strong>13 of 14 cells lose or tie</strong> (marginal 1.04–1.95×, e2e 1.07–2.20×); the compiled twins agree. The single &quot;delivers&quot; cell routes to a different executor — a routing artifact, not a dispatch win.</li><li>mechanism: the spec-walk costs more than the N−1 dispatches it removes, at every measured size. There is no fused kernel.</li><li>consequence for the ledger: the <code>pay</code> leg for this family was <strong>cost-model only</strong>. The referee <em>priced</em> the move and the price was wrong about the executor.</li></ul><p>This is now tracked as tech debt (<code>TODO</code> → &quot;synop* real delivery&quot;) and it is the sharpest open problem in the domain: <strong>a referee that prices can be gamed by its own cost model.</strong></p><p><strong>Update, same night — the diagnosis was right.</strong> <code>prim_kernel_probe</code> gave six of the pointwise prims a generated <code>triton.jit</code> body and timed it against the shipped binding on CUDA fp32 (1 M elements): <strong>0.371–0.392× the binding — 2.55–2.70× faster</strong>, every op verified (<code>graphopt/docs/reports/prim_kernel.md</code>). So the fold never was slow; the <em>interpreter</em> was. The shipped path is fp64 on CPU, where Triton cannot run, which is why the module-level delivery probe sees a loss.</p><h2 id="_4-a-second-domain-and-the-referee-that-measures" tabindex="-1">4. A second domain, and the referee that measures <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-a-second-domain-and-the-referee-that-measures" aria-label="Permalink to “4. A second domain, and the referee that measures”">​</a></h2><p><code>kernelopt/</code> (private; numbers do not leave it) — <code>paper/paper.qmd</code>, <code>docs/results_kernel_check.md</code>, <code>docs/results_overnight_kernel_v2.md</code>.</p><ul><li>26 codegen laws; three-legged referee: certificate, verify-first numeric equality in the sink, <strong>and measured wall time</strong> against a hand-Triton field baseline.</li><li>11-case audit, CUDA fp32: verified-equal fused composites <strong>1.1–5.0×</strong> over spelled, at-or-near the Triton rows; <code>kernel_flat</code> control 1.00×; two mm-epilogue rows drifted, ordering holds.</li><li>learned linear player beats the scripted playbook, held-out: <strong>0.560 vs 0.200</strong> reward/episode.</li><li>headline: a 145-node carrier the sink could not lower at all → a learned schedule fold emits a fused chunked-scan plan at <strong>0.271 ms</strong> vs <strong>0.454 ms</strong> incumbent (~0.28 ms FLA, carried retro).</li><li>caveats carried in the paper: realised under <strong>CUDA-graph replay</strong> (eager is Python-bound); parity on a launch-bound term.</li></ul><p>Reading: the domain whose referee <em>times</em> produces delivery wins; the domain whose referee <em>prices</em> produces them on paper only. Same game layer, same seat, different score.</p><h2 id="_5-the-boundary-—-priors-do-not-move-worlds" tabindex="-1">5. The boundary — priors do not move worlds <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-boundary-—-priors-do-not-move-worlds" aria-label="Permalink to “5. The boundary — priors do not move worlds”">​</a></h2><p><code>kernelopt/docs/results_transfer.md</code> (Q5 remainder):</p><ul><li><strong>10 of 12</strong> foreign policy/domain pairs cannot enumerate a single legal move; the enumerators are structurally typed to their own state.</li><li>the <code>open</code> vocabulary shares <strong>zero</strong> feature names with any other domain.</li><li>the one live channel is <code>a:r:*</code>, the rule-name hash bucket — same hash, different vocabularies; <code>search → kernel</code> reaches 96.8 % live mass and is semantically scrambled by construction.</li><li>every transplant ≤ <strong>0.39×</strong> of the domain&#39;s own prior; native kernel greedy <strong>+0.653</strong> vs cold-greedy <strong>−0.129</strong>.</li></ul><p>So Q5 is <strong>NULL, sharpened</strong>: what transfers is the <em>game</em> (world, referee, score, seat); what does not is the <em>vocabulary and featurization</em>. A prior is a function of a featurization.</p><h2 id="_6-the-player-—-quit-became-a-channel" tabindex="-1">6. The player — quit became a channel <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-the-player-—-quit-became-a-channel" aria-label="Permalink to “6. The player — quit became a channel”">​</a></h2><p><code>docs/results_stop_isolated.md</code> (10-20) → <code>results_stop_gate.md</code> (10-24) → <code>results_stop_verdict.md</code> + <code>results_evidence_gate.md</code> (10-25) → <code>results_open_gated_seat.md</code> (10-25).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>arm</th><th>reward</th><th>AP</th><th>stops</th><th>evals/ep</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>incumbent</td><td>0.2768</td><td>0.2074</td><td>15</td><td>15.28</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>vhead:baked</code> / shipped <code>open-gated</code> seat</strong></td><td><strong>0.2893</strong></td><td><strong>0.2101</strong></td><td><strong>26</strong></td><td>14.56</td></tr><tr><td>never-stop control</td><td>0.2850</td><td>0.2101</td><td>0</td><td>16.00</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>the stop decision is <strong>pure data</strong> and the shipped seat reproduces the measured arm digit for digit — gate as data is proven, not asserted.</li><li>bake bars: reward ≥ 0.273 ∧ AP ≥ 0.210; never-stop arms are labelled confounds in the same table.</li><li><strong>verdict-visible evidence transfers</strong>: <code>vd_live</code> posts decision-row AP <strong>0.631</strong> (0.695 under the baked ordering) — the first column past the ~0.40 ceiling every earlier family hit; trained heads reach held-out usefulness AP 0.449–0.535.</li><li>round 12: the composition <strong>mostly fails</strong> — fitted verdict-spec gates over-stop; one thin honest clear (<code>gate:vd-headroom:baked</code>, 0.2857 / 0.2101, 8 stops).</li><li>new: <code>mlopt_discovery.learned_propose</code> — 406 labelled candidate cells, 14.5 % oracle-pass base rate, but 82–100 % for structured families vs <strong>6.5 %</strong> for blind enumeration. Shipped as a <strong>prior, never a verdict</strong>.</li></ul><p>Unchanged and re-earned: 212 bad accepts refused, four false twins rejected, the fabricated &quot;1.98×&quot; caught, one real lowering bug found.</p><h2 id="_7-architecture-—-the-two-decisions-the-data-forced" tabindex="-1">7. Architecture — the two decisions the data forced <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-architecture-—-the-two-decisions-the-data-forced" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Architecture — the two decisions the data forced”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>ADR-0157 / plan 0033 — the fragment tower.</strong> Vocabularies become <code>Language</code> fragments; minting is fragment emission; growth is a fragment merge. Phases 0–5 in <code>project/plans/0033-fragment-tower.md</code>, each shipping standalone value. Phase 0 (canonical fragment dir + emit API) has no external dependency and is the generator fix. Stated falsifier: <em>if the flagship stack cannot live on the fragment tower, the language-as-data claim is unproven on its hardest corpus.</em></li><li><strong>ADR-0158 — effects resume in flight.</strong> <code>perform</code> dispatches to the innermost covering handler and its return value <em>is</em> the result; the old trace semantics become the <em>unhandled</em> case; stateful handlers ride the calculus&#39; own <code>loop</code>/trace; multi-shot <code>resume</code> deferred.</li><li>Supporting: ADR-0154 (typecheck semantics on an NBE engine), ADR-0152 (nominal inductive families), ADR-0153 (grades as arrow indices), <code>reify_ast</code> (Val→Ast quote half), CI off Dagger onto the bupha runner with smoke/verify layers.</li><li>Artifacts: platform paper (<code>eqsat/paper</code>, 2026-10-21), tensor-domain paper (<code>paper/</code>), private codegen-domain paper.</li></ul><h2 id="_8-evidence-table-—-one-claim-one-artifact" tabindex="-1">8. Evidence table — one claim, one artifact <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-evidence-table-—-one-claim-one-artifact" aria-label="Permalink to “8. Evidence table — one claim, one artifact”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>claim</th><th>artifact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>minted ops are original by construction</td><td><code>docs/reports/primitives_finds.md</code> (21/21, 48/48)</td></tr><tr><td>composition inside a fixed bank cannot be original</td><td><code>kernelopt/docs/results_overnight_kernel_v2.md</code> (97 admitted, 0 original)</td></tr><tr><td>vocabulary bootstraps depth</td><td><code>results_overnight_prim_depth3.md</code>, <code>results_overnight_prim_bootstrap.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>the new family does not pay in wall clock</td><td><code>docs/reports/prim_delivery.md</code> (13/14 lose)</td></tr><tr><td>a measuring referee produces delivery</td><td><code>kernelopt/docs/results_kernel_check.md</code> (1.1–5.0×)</td></tr><tr><td>priors do not transfer</td><td><code>kernelopt/docs/results_transfer.md</code> (10/12, ≤0.39×)</td></tr><tr><td>the stop gate is data and ships</td><td><code>docs/results_open_gated_seat.md</code> (digit-for-digit)</td></tr><tr><td>evidence-based stop transfers</td><td><code>docs/results_stop_verdict.md</code> (0.631)</td></tr><tr><td>the vocabulary is still hand-drawn</td><td><code>project/plans/0033-fragment-tower.md</code> (Phase 0 open)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_9-what-we-are-not-claiming" tabindex="-1">9. What we are <em>not</em> claiming <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-what-we-are-not-claiming" aria-label="Permalink to “9. What we are not claiming”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>not</strong> &quot;127 laws&quot;: 127 ledger <em>names</em> = 13 promoted + 57 folds + 57 premises.</li><li><strong>not</strong> a speed win for the minted family — the opposite, measured.</li><li><strong>not</strong> a reusable player — the transfer test says no.</li><li><strong>not</strong> that the self-modifying game has been shown to <em>improve play</em>. What is shown: the bank grows under play, and a player ranks candidates 4.7× better than the heuristic. The loop between them — play grows the bank, the grown bank makes the <em>same</em> player better without retraining — is still <strong>unmeasured</strong>. That is the G* claim, and it remains open.</li></ul><h2 id="_10-what-next-—-ranked-by-risk-removed-per-week" tabindex="-1">10. What next — ranked by risk removed per week <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-what-next-—-ranked-by-risk-removed-per-week" aria-label="Permalink to “10. What next — ranked by risk removed per week”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Plan 0033 Phase 0 — canonical fragment vocabulary</strong> (no external dependency). Emit <code>ops/laws/handlers/schemas/prims/families</code> from their five current owners behind one schema pin; registries become projections. <em>Pass:</em> an added fragment (human or machine) is visible to <code>pipeline.propose</code> and <code>mlopt-handl-peer.transform_ids</code> identically.</li><li><strong>Make <code>pay</code> measured, then re-admit.</strong> Either build the fused-body codegen path for minted ops (<code>genkernel</code> already lowers <code>claim</code>ed handlers) or gate <code>MACHINE_SHIPPED</code> on measured pay. <strong>Landed 2026-10-25:</strong> <code>bench.referees.pay_wall</code> (graphopt <code>523ab1d</code>) times the delivered module, with a same-executor rule. Verdict: <strong>7 lose · 1 tie · 6 confounded · 0 honest delivers</strong>, and every fold that fires (9 of 57) loses, worst 2.66× (<code>docs/reports/pay_measured.md</code>). So the pass criterion is met the honest way — the family is demoted as a <em>pay claim</em>, kept as provenance. Still open: the 48 unmeasured folds, and whether a real fused body can beat Inductor at these sizes (the compiled twins say no).</li><li><strong>The decisive experiment: does a grown bank improve the same player?</strong> Freeze a player, grow the bank, replay the frozen player on the same held-out boards. <em>Pass:</em> a delta with a paired control. <strong>Measured 2026-10-25</strong> (<code>bench/drivers/policy/bank_growth.py</code>, <code>docs/results_bank_growth.md</code>, 5 seeds, frozen linear player): <ul><li>minted prim growth 74 → 131: <strong>−0.023</strong> reward/episode (all seeds ≤ +0.008) — unpaid moves dilute a fixed policy;</li><li>control, paying growth 127 → 131 (<code>id_add</code>/<code>id_mul</code>/<code>double_neg</code>/ <code>silu_fold</code>): <strong>+0.066</strong> (all seeds ≥ +0.036), ~9× the reward. So <strong>growth helps iff it pays</strong> — the same condition the measured pay gate enforces. G* is not &quot;the bank grows&quot;; it is &quot;the bank grows with moves that pay&quot;. Still to run: mint <em>during</em> play, then measure.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Held-out replication of 21/21.</strong> Mint on <code>zoo-prim</code>, admit on a model family never in the mint corpus. <em>Pass:</em> originals survive at a stated rate, or the corpus dependence is named.</li><li><strong>Close the tech debt that bank growth will break:</strong> import-time domain registration, pinned rule-count tests, kernel-prim shape gate. Cheap now, expensive at 200 rules.</li><li><strong>Un-pin the delivery path from CPU.</strong> <code>torch_bridge.Plan.run_xs</code> allocates intermediates from a CPU <code>like</code> tensor, so a generated Triton body cannot be measured end to end — the op-level 2.6× stands, the module-level number does not exist yet. <code>measure_module</code> now takes <code>device</code>/<code>dtype</code>; the harness (<code>prim_kernel_module</code>) is written and blocked on that one change. This is the highest-value refactor left: it turns &quot;the folds pay&quot; from an op-level result into a delivered one.</li></ol><p><strong>Decision on the 48 unmeasured folds (2026-10-25):</strong> accept them as <em>vocabulary</em>, not as pay claims. They are labeled <code>unmeasured</code> in <code>provenance.pay_status()</code> and in <code>docs/reports/pay_coverage.md</code>; measuring each on its own bound site is a follow-up, not a blocker, because a fold that never fires cannot pay on the corpus that matters.</p><p>Deferred on purpose: scale (Q4 already names the three walls — matcher ~11k nodes, verify-forward RAM, fp64 overflow), and any new domain until the pay leg is honest.</p><h2 id="_11-one-line-summary" tabindex="-1">11. One-line summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#_11-one-line-summary" aria-label="Permalink to “11. One-line summary”">​</a></h2><p><strong>The machine can now name new things and prove them — 127 names, 21 of 21 original — and the only two things still missing are a vocabulary that lives as data and a referee that measures instead of prices.</strong></p>`,41)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};